305
00:11:25.210 --> 00:11:27.850
Fast fact number one, fan question from Maddie Jensen.

306
00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:34.890
I noticed that Ken Kuapis directed the finale for season three, along with the finale of season two.

307
00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:39.310
Both episodes are crucial to the Jim-Pam plotline of the show.

308
00:11:39.530 --> 00:11:46.310
Was this an intentional decision to have the same director in charge of these important episodes, or was it just a happy accident?

309
00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:48.270
Well, this was very intentional.

310
00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:52.030
I'm so glad you said that, because I was sitting here, and I wasn't sure.

311
00:11:52.250 --> 00:11:54.490
I was like, what is she going to say?

312
00:11:54.750 --> 00:12:01.910
And I was like, I hope it was thought out, because Ken was so caring about the different dynamics and relationships on the show.

313
00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:03.310
He had been with us since the pilot.

314
00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:05.790
We had such a trust for his vision.

315
00:12:05.790 --> 00:12:10.690
And if you were about to say, it was just a scheduling thing, I was going to be like, no.

316
00:12:12.210 --> 00:12:20.610
No, I traded some emails with Kent Zabornak, and he told me that he and Greg always wanted Ken Kuapis to direct milestone episodes.

317
00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:29.390
So, season openers, season closers, or any episodes with big story movement, especially if they involve Jim and Pam.

318
00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:32.470
He wasn't always available, but that was the intention.

319
00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:45.510
And if you look back, Ange, you'll see that oftentimes it was Ken Kuapis, Greg Daniels, Paul Feig, or Paul Lieberstein who would direct these big milestone episodes,

320
00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:52.410
which makes sense, because those guys were kind of the original team of visionaries for the show.

321
00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:54.970
And they were with us since the first season.

322
00:12:55.230 --> 00:12:55.370
Yeah.

323
00:12:55.550 --> 00:12:59.290
So, they knew every character's journey, and I think that was super important.

324
00:12:59.290 --> 00:13:02.530
That was sometimes a learning curve for new directors.

325
00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:10.730
If they hadn't seen every single episode, they would come in and maybe not understand why a character was making a certain choice.

326
00:13:10.910 --> 00:13:14.230
And I remember the cast would sometimes just bring them up to speed.

327
00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:15.390
Oh, I did.

328
00:13:16.270 --> 00:13:16.710
Yeah.

329
00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:17.710
I remember I was in a scene.

330
00:13:17.710 --> 00:13:22.810
I forget who the director was, but they were like, so let's have Angela over there, like, cutting the meat or something.

331
00:13:22.810 --> 00:13:24.470
It was like a party episode.

332
00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:26.290
And I was like, oh, she wouldn't touch that meat.

333
00:13:26.390 --> 00:13:27.090
She's a vegetarian.

334
00:13:27.350 --> 00:13:28.790
She wouldn't go near it.

335
00:13:29.290 --> 00:13:29.830
Yes.

336
00:13:30.150 --> 00:13:33.570
And they're like, oh, okay, I guess I'll put you over there by the bag of chips.

337
00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:34.650
I'm like, yes, that's better.

338
00:13:35.790 --> 00:13:36.310
Exactly.

339
00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:44.750
So, this created a really nice sense of consistency for those big episodes to bring people back who knew us all so well.

340
00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:45.570
All right.

341
00:13:45.690 --> 00:13:47.590
Fast fact number two.

342
00:13:47.590 --> 00:13:56.310
This is a fan question from Beatrix Smith, Brooke B., Amelia C., Erica P., and Supa Nanduri.

343
00:13:56.630 --> 00:14:07.210
Did the conversation at the beach between Jim and Pam that we see throughout this episode, did it have to be recreated like the kiss on Casino Night?

344
00:14:07.210 --> 00:14:11.390
Or was it a deleted scene that was added later without audio?

345
00:14:11.390 --> 00:14:12.550
Leo, what's the story?

346
00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:17.270
Well, guys, this was a new scene.

347
00:14:17.490 --> 00:14:17.950
Mm-hmm.

348
00:14:17.950 --> 00:14:21.650
But we knew about it when we were shooting beach games.

349
00:14:21.650 --> 00:14:25.710
So, we shot this during the beach games shoot.

350
00:14:25.830 --> 00:14:28.570
We did not have to go back to the dam.

351
00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:29.110
Oh, yeah.

352
00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:30.470
We couldn't get that location again.

353
00:14:30.470 --> 00:14:32.150
So, they shot it while we were there.

354
00:14:32.370 --> 00:14:35.950
And, Jenna, I remember that they brought Ken Quapis to direct that.

355
00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:37.970
So, and that was actually very common.

356
00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:40.550
That wasn't like anything weird to Harold Ramis.

357
00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:42.530
That was really common on a show.

358
00:14:42.530 --> 00:14:52.890
If you had a cross-board moment where you had a scene that was in another episode, the director of that episode would come and direct that to sort of complete their story.

359
00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:54.510
So, Ken came back.

360
00:14:54.630 --> 00:15:02.030
And, Jenna, I read that this was supposed to be in the beginning of the show, like the cold open.

361
00:15:02.030 --> 00:15:10.330
Yes, just like that jump from Casino Night to Gay Witch Hunt, we were going to originally open this episode.

362
00:15:10.530 --> 00:15:15.990
The cold open was going to be watching this scene between Jim and Pam on the beach.

363
00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:17.830
I'm so glad it wasn't that way.

364
00:15:18.350 --> 00:15:21.730
I think it creates so much more tension.

365
00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:22.570
Yeah.

366
00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:26.190
To leave it until later as a flashback.

367
00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:34.850
And, you know, Jenna, it really did add to the tension because now we know as the audience that they had had this moment.

368
00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:42.330
And so, when Pam was saying goodbye and good luck and all of that, it's layered with this moment that they shared at beach games.

369
00:15:42.630 --> 00:15:44.690
And I love that our show did this.

370
00:15:44.810 --> 00:15:47.070
Our show didn't always just spoon-feed the audience.

371
00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:49.950
It just had them, like, sort of pick up in the middle.

372
00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:51.430
And I just loved it.

373
00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:52.550
Me too.

374
00:15:52.730 --> 00:15:53.270
Me too.

375
00:15:53.270 --> 00:15:59.190
It was during the editing process that they decided to take it out of the cold open and put it later.

376
00:15:59.590 --> 00:16:06.470
Dave Rogers said in the DVD commentary that it was Mike Schur's idea to make it a flashback later in the episode.

377
00:16:07.090 --> 00:16:07.870
I love that.

378
00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:08.270
I know.

379
00:16:08.370 --> 00:16:08.950
He's so smart.

380
00:16:09.190 --> 00:16:10.130
He's such a smarty.

